DO  - 10.1016/j.molmet.2026.102468
AB  - Loss of lean mass during weight reduction may compromise metabolic health and physical function. Urocortin 2 (UCN2) activates corticotropin-releasing factor receptor 2 (CRF2) and has been linked to beneficial effects on skeletal muscle and glucose metabolism. We evaluated an acylated UCN2 analogue (acyUCN2), alone or combined with an acylated GLP-1 analogue (acyGLP-1), in preclinical obesity models. Diet-induced obese (DIO) Sprague Dawley rats and DIO Göttingen Minipigs were treated with vehicle or acyUCN2 (rats, pigs) or acyGLP-1 or combination therapy (rats only). Food intake, body weight (BW), body composition, glucose tolerance and energy expenditure (rats only) were assessed. Histology and RNA-sequencing were used to characterize muscle remodelling and transcriptional responses. In rats, acyUCN2 reduced food intake and fat mass, increased lean mass and muscle weight, and improved glucose tolerance despite limited effects on BW. Energy expenditure was not affected. Histology demonstrated hypertrophy across fibre types and a shift from type 2A to type 2X/B fibres. Transcriptomic analyses supported muscle growth and fibre type switch, relative reduced vasculature, regulation of neuromuscular junction-associated genes, and overlap to human acute exercise-response signatures. In obese minipigs, acyUCN2 increased lean mass and improved insulin sensitivity, with modest effects on BW and fat mass, alongside regulation of genes linked to muscle biology and metabolism. Pharmacological CRF2 agonism promotes favourable body composition, muscle hypertrophy, and improved glucose metabolism despite limited weight loss, supporting clinical development of UCN2 analogues as a differentiated obesity treatment, particularly in combination with appetite-regulating therapies and in settings where preservation of functional muscle mass is critical.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42966498/
